{
  "requirement": "A definition glob MUST match a complete full name. `*` MUST match zero or more characters, including dots; all other valid name characters MUST match literally. An empty glob MUST be invalid. Every definition selection MUST use this matching rule, including requirements evidence inputs, CLI definition filters, and the authoring `references` tool, and MUST match against both requirements and named examples.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The tests adequately cover `requirements.references.definition_globs`. They check the matching rule and each place the rule must apply.\n\n**Matching rule**\n- `src/definition_glob.rs#mod[tests]/fn[wildcard_crosses_dot_segments_and_matches_the_whole_name]` checks that matching is anchored to the whole name. A prefix or suffix on `foo.*.bar` fails, and `foo.bar.*` does not match `foo.bar`.\n- The same test checks that `*` crosses dots: `foo.*.bar` matches both `foo.x.bar` and `foo.x.y.bar`.\n- It checks that other characters match literally. `.` is not a regex wildcard (`foo.bar` against `fooxbar`), and `_` and `-` are literal.\n- It checks that an empty glob is invalid (`validate(\"\")` errors) and that an unsupported character is invalid (`foo[bar]`).\n- Zero-character `*` matches are exercised in `tests/commands.rs#fn[commands_report_invalid_arguments_and_unmatched_selectors]`. There `requirements.*ready` matches both `requirements.ready` and `requirements.xready`.\n\n**Every selection path uses the rule**\n- **Evidence inputs:** `src/evidence.rs#mod[tests]/fn[requirements_inputs_track_matched_declarations_in_full_name_order]` covers the dot-crossing glob `foo.*.bar`. It matches both a requirement and an example, and it also covers a glob that matches nothing. It rejects `foo.[a].bar` and `\"\"` as invalid.\n- **Authoring `habeas` requirements reads:** `src/authoring/tools.rs#mod[tests]/fn[requirements_reads_select_requirements_and_examples_but_not_definitions]` and `tests/definitions.rs#fn[selections_match_requirements_and_examples_but_never_named_definitions]` check that `requirements.*` or `rules.*` selects the requirement and the example. A term whose name also matches is not selected, and an exact definition name errors.\n- **Authoring `references` tool:** `src/authoring/tools.rs#mod[tests]/fn[references_list_names_and_flattened_sites_without_recording_inputs]` checks that `requirements.*` returns both requirements and the example, and that `\"\"` and `bad name` are rejected. The `ripgrep` and `ast_grep` `referencing` tests use globs and reject a missing selection.\n- **CLI `status` and `info`:** `tests/commands.rs#fn[commands_report_invalid_arguments_and_unmatched_selectors]` checks that `[` is rejected as an invalid glob, that an unmatched glob fails, and that `requirements.*ready` matches. `tests/definitions.rs#fn[selections_match_requirements_and_examples_but_never_named_definitions]` checks `rules.*` for both commands and that `info rules.used` fails.\n\n**Empty glob on the CLI**\n- No test passes an empty glob to the CLI directly. A search of the source shows `info_markdown` and `status_markdown_with_coverage` call the same `definition_glob::validate`. The empty-glob check is tested at the shared function, in evidence inputs and in the references tool. I do not count this as a gap.",
  "references": "e33afeaa4a252d265fbc645047b6ff36bea7de48291c4c2dd1b57ee30dd597e3",
